#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161803 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162011 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6832be-62e1-506c-9b78-685f65362117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "42777bd2-44d0-5117-9e8b-ee722d8f3229" :question/math-academy-id "e-8276" :question/problem "Find the principal axes of the curve $19x_1^2-6x_1x_2+11x_2^2 = 20.$" :question/worked-solution "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=19x_1^2-6x_1x_2+11x_2^2.$ The matrix of the form is \n\n![](images/31/31f8c54fecdb8b2e8b3f3cad20a642a39eab9ae2987d49849a1c1dabc34e5368.png)\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = 20\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I| = 0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=20 \\quad$ and $\\quad \\lambda_2=10.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=20,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-20I & =\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{bmatrix}-20\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-20I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/51/51897cac20d424f599eba1c8aada5259279ab9ad263e7557d6614ce79e68fa0f.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{10},$ we get \n![](images/f9/f9913f9139af447be912bccc2350f086ce32bae89849495f8e82a3eebf8a7918.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/2c/2cdd8527be173b17e254bd4ecda571d71b15938c3a1f6cb2609122db2b10c64d.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{10},$ we get \n![](images/f6/f6764ef3200afce932740e8100915018b6a0c21e902d932b218e79bcd57e0b95.png)\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\qquad$ ![](images/15/1549a73afb90f3fa0fb8218d9d63daa1a5534c3e546613cbf3f226371b8f1092.png) and ![](images/d4/d4a6e3f0c138e6dd83252df8adc14359ba67ae77dec0270264ea6d00c7b08667.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161804 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162012 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5d6ac06-40b4-5da0-bd26-860b35826292" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "3564c452-1a51-5455-a6b1-223fda1611c0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8277" :question/problem "Which curve is defined by the equation $6x_1^2+8x_1x_2-9x_2^2= 70?$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=6x_1^2+8x_1x_2-9x_2^2.$ To  determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ which is\n\n![](images/33/33a30f2c07875359a60caed74f802a2de29ac9e44089800ed8f30a2015303bce.png)\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -70 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =7,\\,-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $7y_1^2 -10 y_2^2.$  \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{y}_{1}^{2}-10{y}_{2}^{2} & =70 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{10}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{7} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{10})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{10}$ and $\\sqrt{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161815 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162023 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf2e573c-f351-5d5a-8e59-9c6532f1ab9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Curve Using a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "ec194ea7-df30-5370-b0c4-d27d4b34f8c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-8473" :question/problem "Draw a diagram that depicts the lines representing the principal axes of the quadratic curve shown below.\n\n![](images/53/5354b2fa6d2fb2c8b1b9ea3cb675225619e8debe64e1ec108b556248d6e672ec.png)" :question/worked-solution "The principal axes of a curve define a coordinate system in which the curve can be represented by its (simplest) canonical equation. \n\nOur curve is a hyperbola. This curve will have the canonical equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nif one axis passes through the vertices of the hyperbola and the other axis is the perpendicular bisector of the segment connecting the two vertices.\n\n![](images/bd/bd0d117543012be1c4eab0acda878c9aea67518e46c93048e8c249de1a0c03e6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161838 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162044 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e97b8e36-3790-5cdc-bd77-417574af23f3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve Given the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "ba46e82f-10b0-529c-94b0-3aebb7d1e1a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8678" :question/problem "Consider the matrix ![](images/41/4114e6536f57106949ed39a6ae4979646a0dd58c5690d7e7bcf9ffed15de8086.png) and the quadratic curve $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = 1.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=1,$ find the principal axes of the curve." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = 1\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/77/771d020606de64641ab305039a0c9a9de926080e83707b270a333b5775a8e028.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](images/54/54a77383e863630dfd4e21a796801001aaed3084f1097bca62ef4a098e20b405.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/e0/e0d71ad9216b9fa7c1831e6874b9a97bba9c1d9c5dbd6d9664635ed468d82a4c.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](images/83/83fd398b476c1c749346754a05c7f4071a98ab61453405a99ad8fcff65e38aaf.png)\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\qquad$ ![](images/8f/8f0d84bb5c3ae37714c781b5dafbba1ae7de8561234ca558d3da2dcbfa9d75cf.png) and ![](images/29/293ad98697d595e3e0049aae93e54209b6d17aa92eb131b57791f211167690fc.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162044 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e97b8e36-3790-5cdc-bd77-417574af23f3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve Given the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bc8535e-5191-506c-9469-773d7d938c90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363191} {:answer/id #uuid "46271119-ca01-5f58-8795-ae0f52785730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363192} {:answer/id #uuid "fb40d380-5dc7-5e14-9da4-4417962eb0dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363193} {:answer/id #uuid "d8830562-3dcc-5248-a8aa-c5cdee386516" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363194} {:answer/id #uuid "608033c7-43b2-5dcf-9f24-fcebddc5bc0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8830562-3dcc-5248-a8aa-c5cdee386516" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363194} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce51ee15-fc58-56a6-9c5e-720225157d7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "778909b3-51d4-53f8-86d3-fe36ffdc7654" :question/math-academy-id "q-140551" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=11.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ find the principal axes of the curve." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=11\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-5)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}+5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-5)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162012 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5d6ac06-40b4-5da0-bd26-860b35826292" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "645a7e0b-2e3d-5867-b381-6a1443ab619a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411437} {:answer/id #uuid "87d10ffd-ae37-597e-8619-29681e2d4321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411442} {:answer/id #uuid "cc8968ee-c0f9-5a40-a746-8ba2c518a9ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411451} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0e0014-393a-5d8a-a0b7-5361c1d979fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411452} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e73c4f-2431-51f3-9280-279505b4a83a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87d10ffd-ae37-597e-8619-29681e2d4321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411442} :answer-field/id #uuid "66f12865-3f75-50a9-82af-d153989de8e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8d1da1f-2237-5249-8b88-e74004bb86c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94985" :question/problem "Which curve is defined by the equation $9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}=10?$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.  \n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}9-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-15\\lambda +50 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $5{y}_{1}^{2}+10{y}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}+10{y}_{2}^{2} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{1} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{1} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404752 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162012 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5d6ac06-40b4-5da0-bd26-860b35826292" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffef5c19-fd74-5ba0-af4d-7b043c0b9bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404754} {:answer/id #uuid "f3838d79-b19d-50cb-bda4-8a032786b6e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404755} {:answer/id #uuid "c768ce44-3d75-5d9d-b7e6-297ae3bec501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circle of radius $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404756} {:answer/id #uuid "0ddd23b0-eb68-5321-80ed-2e347cdf3572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $3$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404757} {:answer/id #uuid "bff4817a-3051-5961-a312-060e27c50a46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffef5c19-fd74-5ba0-af4d-7b043c0b9bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404754} :answer-field/id #uuid "23ec9caf-4548-5197-b9d1-b78a71376df8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "524143ac-0f98-5858-ae7f-d1be1f96a542" :question/math-academy-id "q-94997" :question/problem "Which curve is defined by the equation $5{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}=12?$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=5{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}5-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +24 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =6,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $6{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162012 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5d6ac06-40b4-5da0-bd26-860b35826292" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24fa485f-91ea-5bd7-b5bf-ff53f7921b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A  hyperbola with semiaxes  $2$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411433} {:answer/id #uuid "40462880-f937-54f8-9c63-a892cafb32da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes  $2$ and $\\sqrt{8}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411435} {:answer/id #uuid "69cb5485-7267-5d3c-8e9b-6410002fdbd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes  $\\sqrt{8}$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411438} {:answer/id #uuid "a1267461-8286-578d-8e73-1197cd603858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circle with radius  $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411446} {:answer/id #uuid "efcf49e3-8778-553a-9158-db5800030dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Two parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24fa485f-91ea-5bd7-b5bf-ff53f7921b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A  hyperbola with semiaxes  $2$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411433} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fdaee3e-396b-5d40-981f-615ed5526c05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6abf962b-d107-56b7-9263-80678639c14a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95002" :question/problem "Which curve is defined by the equation $3{x}_{1}^{2}+10{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}=32?$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+10{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.  \n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 5 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda -16 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =8,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $8{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}.$  \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2} & =32 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola with semiaxes  $2$ and $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162012 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5d6ac06-40b4-5da0-bd26-860b35826292" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daccfdf3-d1f7-5797-9ac9-314280e5bc3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes   $2$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364539} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9f6695-dc48-55d9-b4cf-893b2f96259b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circle with radius $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364540} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb69aff-6117-53ed-9a1a-e5e1f304bf14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Two parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364541} {:answer/id #uuid "0afb28a8-64c7-564f-9f0a-b9a1c3268f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A ellipse with semiaxes   $2$ and $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364542} {:answer/id #uuid "2950bc01-46f5-5cf6-8d09-bc69e25f476f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes   $2$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2950bc01-46f5-5cf6-8d09-bc69e25f476f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes   $2$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364543} :answer-field/id #uuid "025724f9-8596-5228-8525-a32f3fb963b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94abbaa5-1aca-5296-9230-1760b889fa9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95011" :question/problem "Which curve is defined by the equation $24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}=100?$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.  \n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}24 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 21\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}24-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 21-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-45\\lambda +500 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =25,\\,20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2}.$  \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2} & =100 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes   $2$ and $\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162011 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6832be-62e1-506c-9b78-685f65362117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4db011e7-e8e1-5d13-a5da-6e25ab114bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358422} {:answer/id #uuid "4f36a6f0-0306-53ce-9f3b-f51a8e8c1107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358436} {:answer/id #uuid "59183542-0c9a-5496-9310-4b537893ba5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358527} {:answer/id #uuid "85e3cceb-cf77-5880-ac58-91067d2b9189" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358928} {:answer/id #uuid "ad45d6d4-f44d-5ead-9834-2cb2e4d1d813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f36a6f0-0306-53ce-9f3b-f51a8e8c1107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358436} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f48b255-f6e3-52e3-91b0-049f03c6a590" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "82f29e7a-6602-5687-8c17-e73a53aea769" :question/math-academy-id "q-95058" :question/problem "Find the principal axes of the curve $24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}=100.$" :question/worked-solution "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}24 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 21\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=100\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=25\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=20.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-25I & =\\begin{bmatrix}24 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 21\\end{bmatrix}-25\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-25I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=20,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-20I & =\\begin{bmatrix}24 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 21\\end{bmatrix}-20\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-20I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360631 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162011 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6832be-62e1-506c-9b78-685f65362117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78888e47-630e-5d10-83dd-c01e1f6d8f8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358791} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed54b28-985c-5f59-9dd6-8f64af5272ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358858} {:answer/id #uuid "ad915cb2-2d1c-55a0-8c8c-777c7b585870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359254} {:answer/id #uuid "e10192f8-349a-539e-9073-a30553a0d1a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359734} {:answer/id #uuid "e2427fc5-316a-5fa7-80a0-6cf2a0c6ea10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2427fc5-316a-5fa7-80a0-6cf2a0c6ea10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359753} :answer-field/id #uuid "a08665b1-cc8f-56e9-9105-4ca0c202d740" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e61ec711-f880-5e14-a323-327181d18534" :question/math-academy-id "q-95806" :question/problem "Find the principal axes of the curve $2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}=9.$" :question/worked-solution "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=9\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=0.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162023 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf2e573c-f351-5d5a-8e59-9c6532f1ab9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Curve Using a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ace36a2e-0ec3-5fd6-8e28-e15f71387012" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/54/542ebdbeb263bbee21edd5d07e0c0288d7ed7f577efca2a17456c6d3b9db988c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161487} {:answer/id #uuid "f4a8b1c9-f82e-5cab-be72-0fb9e8f037a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4c/4c78dbb5109603ccad77b25ff299b319f06338e0c34702d4a5e46991205646dd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161488} {:answer/id #uuid "0c648342-e0a7-54c0-8088-f4c007261690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d9/d9a9302087ebe766a277250d4c41d6d86252d0adbd082fc19399a9023330748e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161489} {:answer/id #uuid "15f8a0b1-1eb4-570b-aaf9-440019de1f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :