dent :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e3c2cae-6be7-5f78-a826-46459817fa90" :question/math-academy-id "q-96727" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e6a6d3891e514f31c77ccbf098d06843bbcd771f2ebea83861683584ec5e8805.png)\n\nWhich of the following diagrams depicts the lines representing the principal axes of the quadratic curve shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The principal axes of a curve define a coordinate system in which the curve can be represented by its (simplest) canonical equation. \n\nOur curve is an ellipse. This curve will have the canonical equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nif the axes pass through the opposite vertices of the ellipse.\n\n![](images/d0/d06b0e6d93c3c0679aea11512963e19c2e641f506558f60c185624de2464c451.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162023 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf2e573c-f351-5d5a-8e59-9c6532f1ab9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Curve Using a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cdf7294-27ad-5c5f-938b-23b5a1e654b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5a/5a7d2c054c664b0c313c120fff332f76d2cd0d4d8152c52ca517e1e2bd5a4622.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395190} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1d5a5e-a88e-59a0-a645-f4eb3ccf5feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/87/878496864c782c63e97799b7d75598abe0bc04da1cc8b0716ea315eec99e378e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395191} {:answer/id #uuid "05aad34f-e824-58fd-8635-d142c9e7b023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6a/6aeb70c5041be20ab9c38724e3cf1e1852f491478c32037222beb83372ab7776.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395192} {:answer/id #uuid "020c5584-4a73-53a6-9b88-b65945011b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ae/ae362c4e6d675c8d7ca65f2aa3702d65c63b0ffb6e370a573da00532e567ab1c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395193} {:answer/id #uuid "42e91f65-6f16-5cdf-9580-e20d5c21cad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/84/8478a27a046a1e4f7d26534b34f7b4c002ff5cb86bd553937b01ebab8ad61d4f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e1d5a5e-a88e-59a0-a645-f4eb3ccf5feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/87/878496864c782c63e97799b7d75598abe0bc04da1cc8b0716ea315eec99e378e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395191} :answer-field/id #uuid "3502f226-41c6-5169-983e-514539e92677" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e348ec9f-ae43-5668-80e5-03590999666c" :question/math-academy-id "q-96798" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bb4f785a62492c7ec1fe8aaecdb998bb6fba6b2a181837f49f328b9782d96548.png)\n\nWhich of the following diagrams depicts the lines representing the principal axes of the quadratic curve shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The principal axes of a curve define a coordinate system in which the curve can be represented by its (simplest) canonical equation. \n\nOur curve is a hyperbola. This curve will have the canonical equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nif one axis passes through the vertices of the hyperbola and the other axis is the perpendicular bisector of the segment connecting the two vertices.\n\n![](images/87/878496864c782c63e97799b7d75598abe0bc04da1cc8b0716ea315eec99e378e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162044 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e97b8e36-3790-5cdc-bd77-417574af23f3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve Given the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "816b00cd-c163-578e-9e67-cd031b1f10af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161613} {:answer/id #uuid "866ab653-dce0-5004-a043-5e8c572bee95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161614} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6e1532-4a17-5c1a-b959-d5f981528176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161615} {:answer/id #uuid "b629b10a-e7f8-56d9-8e10-0dd66fa5859b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161616} {:answer/id #uuid "c92890c5-c66c-5b6e-991c-d638b43650a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161617}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2f62effc-9725-5947-8f6d-28d8e6665441" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161612}] :question/id #uuid "d0a2201d-eedf-5d63-b69f-e84bb28d5179" :question/math-academy-id "q-97987" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$ and the quadratic curve $x^{T}Ax = 5$. Given that $A$ has eigenvalues $λ_{1} = 10$ and $λ_{2} =-3$, find the principal axes of the curve."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162044 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e97b8e36-3790-5cdc-bd77-417574af23f3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve Given the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46150034-989c-516f-93b6-095ee6a1128f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361140} {:answer/id #uuid "7e9550b3-3b43-558a-b687-941423083fde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361394} {:answer/id #uuid "974bf80c-567c-5781-bb2e-8dc105925a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361512} {:answer/id #uuid "9af896d3-e35f-5f59-b9a5-41d2e1a7b105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361527} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d6ae70-5ded-5400-bdbb-443f404b013a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9af896d3-e35f-5f59-b9a5-41d2e1a7b105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361527} :answer-field/id #uuid "c94d3a5d-99de-5470-b87a-285f7cc8b266" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b21820d5-a22a-56c9-af9c-9c1893fbf413" :question/math-academy-id "q-97993" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=3.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7,$ find the principal axes of the curve." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=3\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-7)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}+7\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-7)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162044 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e97b8e36-3790-5cdc-bd77-417574af23f3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve Given the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e022e2a9-7ada-59ab-9b55-0e78c7221355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405183} {:answer/id #uuid "891a2dde-5575-57e6-8a39-eba10064aba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405184} {:answer/id #uuid "36bf5957-c85d-532e-a8e3-8bcd5ef2026b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405185} {:answer/id #uuid "581abcc9-7db9-51b9-92f8-690c96003338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405186} {:answer/id #uuid "a984d186-939c-58fa-963a-f7fbf679d08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "891a2dde-5575-57e6-8a39-eba10064aba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405184} :answer-field/id #uuid "efb02de0-491f-59d6-8a75-57bfea7150b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "091c06c4-0cfd-5952-9df6-eba9b260f93a" :question/math-academy-id "q-97995" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ find unit vectors along the principal axes of the quadratic form." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the quadratic form \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A+4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, unit vectors along the principal axes of our quadratic form are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"}]}
